@meta
id: 25-01
title: Counting from where you are
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-syntax
par: 23
minutes: 4
vscode: click the first line, shift-click the fifth, Delete
open: refund.provider.ts

@brief
Someone left five lines of debugging in `refund` — the `// DEBUG`
comment and the four `console.log` calls under it. All five go. Nothing
else in the file changes.

Chapter 24 gave you ranges made of line numbers, `.`, `$` and `%`. This
chapter finishes the address grammar, starting with the piece that makes
`.` worth having: arithmetic. `.+3` is three lines below the cursor.
`.-1` is the line above it. So a range can say "from here, five lines"
without you reading a single number out of the gutter.

Search down to the first debug line, then delete from there.

@keys
| `:.,.+4d` | from the cursor, five lines, gone |
| `.+1` | the line below the cursor |
| `.-1` | the line above it |
| `:.,.+4` | a range: current line through current plus four |

@hints
Get the cursor onto the `// DEBUG` line first. A search is the cheapest
way there — the word is in capitals and appears once.
--
Now count the lines to remove, including the one you are on: five. The
last of them is `.+4`.
--
`/DEBUG` Enter, then `:.,.+4d` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    /DEBUG<Enter>  :.,.+4d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Nineteen keystrokes. `5dd` would have been shorter here, and on a five-line
block that is the right call. The reason to learn the address form anyway
is that it composes. `5dd` deletes. `:.,.+4` is a *noun* — the same five
lines can be copied with `t`, moved with `m`, indented with `>`, sorted,
substituted in, or handed to `:normal` later in this chapter, and you
write the range the same way every time.

The important half is `.`. An address written with `.` does not care
where in the file you are, so it survives the lines above it changing.
A range of `14,19` is a fact about today's version of the file; `.,.+4`
is a fact about the block, and you can search your way to it.

Offsets work on any address, not just `.`. A bare `+3` means `.+3`, and
`:+3d` deletes the line three below the cursor. You will see the same
arithmetic on `$` next, and on patterns shortly after that.

@start refund.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    // DEBUG remove before merge
    console.log('refund called', orderId);
    console.log('amount', amount);
    console.log('env', process.env.NODE_ENV);
    console.log('gateway', this.paymentService.name);
    const receipt = await this.paymentService.refund(orderId, amount);

    return receipt;
  }
}

@expected refund.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class RefundProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    const receipt = await this.paymentService.refund(orderId, amount);

    return receipt;
  }
}
